@page "/table-large"

<CatHead Title="Large tables">
    A <a href="/table"><code>.table</code></a> with more rows than a page can hold: rendered a window
    at a time, loaded a page at a time, folded into a hierarchy, or narrowed by a filter that says how
    many each choice would leave. The totals stay in view while the rows scroll.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-information-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The table's state is the app's</span>
        The sort, the filters and the page are your query, and you render the rows they return &mdash; keep
        them in your component, your address or your store, wherever the app keeps state. The script
        holds only what the reader does inside the table, and each part of it is readable:
        <code>sednaUi.grid.range(grid)</code> is the selected cells of a
        <a href="/table-editing">keyboard grid</a>, <code>sednaUi.grid.expanded(grid)</code> the open
        branches of a tree grid, and a tree grid says each change with
        <code>sedna-tree-expand</code> and <code>sedna-tree-collapse</code>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.VirtualizedRows" Title="Virtualized rows" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; scroll it.</strong> Blazor's <code>&lt;Virtualize&gt;</code> inside the
        <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code>, with <code>SpacerElement="tr"</code>: the rows in view are in the page,
        and an empty <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code> before and after them stands in for the rest. Nothing in the
        table paints that row &mdash; the zebra stripe counts past it, a stacked table does not make it a
        card, and a keyboard grid never lands on it.
    </p>
    <p>
        Give each row <code>aria-rowindex</code>, its place in the whole table with the header as row 1,
        and the table <code>aria-rowcount</code>. A screen reader is told where the window is, and the
        stripe follows the row's own number rather than its place among the rows rendered, so it does not
        flicker as the window moves. Keep <code>ItemSize</code> near the real row height.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.TableLarge.LoadMore" Title="Loading more" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        For a list the reader pages through on request rather than by scrolling: a <code>.tr-more</code>
        row after the rows, one cell spanning the table with the button and a
        <code>.tr-more-count</code>. While the next page loads, <code>aria-busy="true"</code> on the button
        shows the spinner and stops a second press; keep the focus on it, and append the rows above it.
        The row is never striped. <strong>The buttons here do nothing.</strong>
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.TableLarge.TreeGrid" Title="A tree grid" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; Tab in, then use the arrow keys.</strong> <code>role="treegrid"</code> on the
        table, <code>aria-level</code> on each body row and <code>aria-expanded</code> on a branch; the
        cell holding a <code>.tree-toggle</code> is the tree column, indented a step per level. It is a
        <a href="/table-editing">keyboard grid</a> throughout: on the tree cell <kbd>&rarr;</kbd> opens a
        closed branch and <kbd>&larr;</kbd> closes an open one, then climbs to its parent &mdash; mirrored
        right to left &mdash;, <kbd>Enter</kbd> or a click on the caret opens and closes, and every other
        key moves between cells.
    </p>
    <p>
        The rows stay flat in the <code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code>. The script owns <code>hidden</code> on them:
        a row is shown while every branch above it is open. Render the rows under a closed branch
        <code>hidden</code> for the first paint, as here, or leave them out until the branch opens.
        <code>--treegrid-indent</code> is the step.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.TreeGridLazy" Title="A tree grid from Blazor" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open a year.</strong> The rows under a branch are rendered when it opens.
        <code>data-tree-lazy</code> on a row with none yet makes the open row busy and sends
        <code>Lazy</code> with <code>sedna-tree-expand</code>; the app loads the rows, renders them and
        drops the attribute. <code>@@onsedna-tree-expand</code> and <code>@@onsedna-tree-collapse</code> on
        the table keep the app's own copy of what is open, the same events and arguments as a
        <a href="/tree">tree</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.TableLarge.StickyTotals" Title="Totals and your row, pinned" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.table--sticky-foot</code> holds the <code>&lt;tfoot&gt;</code> at the bottom of the
        scroller as <code>.table--sticky</code> holds the header at the top, so the totals stay in view
        while the rows scroll under them. A <code>.tr-pinned</code> row in the <code>&lt;thead&gt;</code>,
        after the headings, is a record kept in view &mdash; the reader's own store, their place in a
        ranking. Both work with a pinned column, and a keyboard grid keeps the cell it reaches clear of
        them.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.TableLarge.FacetCounts" Title="Filter choices with counts" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A <code>.form-check-count</code> at the end of each choice in a
        <a href="/table"><code>.th-filter</code></a> popover says how many records the choice would leave
        &mdash; the facet counts your query returns beside the rows. The counts line up at the end edge
        in figures of one width. Disable a choice that would leave none, and say how many the filter
        will show on the button that applies it.
    </p>
</CatExample>
